1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:19,980
This is guardrails-lens, the last review pass in the writing loop. Tessl review
run scores it in the terminal: validation checks first, then judges for the
content and the description. It comes back at ninety percent. The description
judge says why. There are no phrases a person would actually use to ask for it,
and some overlap with general editing skills.

2
00:00:20,730 --> 00:00:40,360
Tessl review fix acts on that feedback. It rewrites the skill, reviews the
rewrite, and keeps going until it reaches the target score. Each round takes a
few minutes, so this part is sped up. The skill reached ninety-five percent, and
only the description changed. It now leads with the argument problems this lens
catches, and adds the phrases people use to ask for it.

3
00:00:41,160 --> 00:00:43,830
A fresh review confirms it: ninety-five percent.

4
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:59,650
Now the change goes through the same gate as any other. Push the branch, open
the pull request, and watch the checks. A Tessl skill review runs on every pull
request that touches a skill, next to the plugin validation.

5
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:16,120
On GitHub, the review lands as a single comment on the pull request: a score for
each changed skill, any validation warnings, and the full breakdown from each
judge one click away. Nothing was published to the registry. It all ran in the
repository's own workflow.
